[[File:Illa Daurada waterfront.png|thumb|right|300px|The waterfront of Illa Daurada, the more developed of the two major islands.]]

'''Illa del Portal''' (Volisanian: "Gate Island") sits at the mouth of the Badia de Sant Cristòfor, from which it takes its name — the island serves as the "gate" to the bay. It is roughly circular, with a low, forested profile. The island was used as a quarantine and immigration station from the colonial period through the Zong Balboa era; the former quarantine buildings, now listed as heritage structures, survive on the island's northern shore. Since 1986, the island has been designated a nature reserve and public park, accessible by the [[Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor]] during daylight hours. A small population of wardens and researchers is the island's only permanent residents.

'''Illa Daurada''' (Volisanian: "Golden Island") is the larger of the two islands, long and narrow, stretching along the southern side of the bay. The island is the wealthiest residential area in the comarca, with large estates, luxury hotels, and private beaches along its western (ocean-facing) shore. The eastern (bay-facing) shore is more developed, with a marina, restaurants, and the [[Museu de les Illes]]. The island's name is variously attributed to the golden light at sunset on its ocean-facing cliffs or to a Birú legend about gold artefacts buried on the island. The Municipalitat de les Illes' administrative centre, '''Port Daurat''', is located on the bay-facing shore.

'''Illa de Matarí''' (from Birú *matarí*, "wet land") is a small, flat, marshy island in the inner bay. It is uninhabited and maintained as a public park and bird sanctuary, accessible by ferry from the mainland waterfront.

== Municipalities ==

The comarca is divided into two municipalities, each with its own elected council (''consell municipal'') and mayor (''batlle''):

=== Municipalitat de Sant Cristòfor ===

{{main|Sant Cristòfor}}

The mainland municipality covers the city of Sant Cristòfor and its contiguous urban area, including the Península de la Barceloneta and the mainland shore of the Badia de Sant Cristòfor. It had a population of approximately 5,159,000 in 2026, making it the most populous municipality in Balboa. The municipality is further divided into ''districte''s (districts) for administrative purposes.

{{main|Districts of Sant Cristòfor}}

=== Municipalitat de les Illes ===

The island municipality comprises [[Illa del Portal]], [[Illa Daurada]], and [[Illa de Matarí]]. It had a population of approximately 18,620 in 2026, the vast majority resident on Illa Daurada. The administrative centre is '''Port Daurat''' on Illa Daurada.

The municipality was established in 1964 during the AC mandate period, when the islands were separated from the mainland municipal administration. The separation was driven by the islands' distinct character — Illa Daurada had developed as a wealthy residential enclave with little connection to the mainland city's urban governance, while Illa del Portal was still operating as a quarantine station under federal authority. The creation of a separate municipality allowed the island community to manage its own planning, infrastructure, and services independently from the mainland.

The Municipalitat de les Illes is governed by a nine-member ''consell municipal'' elected every four years. The municipality maintains its own ferry service, the [[Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor]], connecting Port Daurat to the mainland waterfront and to Illa del Portal (for authorised personnel). The municipality also operates the [[Museu de les Illes]], a small museum of island history and natural history, and manages the nature reserve on Illa del Portal.

Illa de Matarí, as an uninhabited public park, falls under the municipality's parks and recreation department but is maintained in partnership with the [[Estret Provincial Parks Authority]].

== Demographics ==

The comarca had a population of 5,178,000 in 2026, making it the most populous comarca in Balboa and one of the most densely populated second-level divisions in Lurandia. The population is overwhelmingly concentrated in the mainland municipality; the island municipality accounts for less than 0.4 per cent of the comarca's total population.

The ethnic composition reflects Sant Cristòfor's history as a cosmopolitan entrepôt. [[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] constitute the largest group, concentrated in the older neighbourhoods and the northern and southern suburbs. [[Fosian Balboan people|Fosian Balboans]], descended from Zong-era immigrants, are concentrated in the historic Fosian quarter and the port area. [[White Balboan people|White Balboans]] are a significant minority in the financial district and the wealthier residential areas, including Illa Daurada. The [[Birú people|indigenous Birú]] population is small but present in the city's traditional market areas.

Illa Daurada is the wealthiest area in the comarca and one of the wealthiest in Balboa; its population is predominantly White and affluent Fosian Balboan, with a small community of international expatriates associated with the nearby [[International City of Areza]].

== Economy ==

{{main|Economy of Balboa}}

The comarca is the economic centre of Balboa and one of the principal financial and logistics hubs in Lurandia. The city's economy is dominated by services — financial services, logistics, trade, and tourism — with the [[Balboa Canal]] and the [[Sant Cristòfor Free Trade Zone]] as the foundation.

The [[Borsa de Sant Cristòfor]], the largest stock exchange in the isthmus region, is located in the Districte Financer. The city's harbour, reconstructed under the AC mandate and expanded after reunification, is one of the busiest ports on the isthmus, handling container transshipment, bulk cargo, and petroleum products. The cruise industry is a major component of the local economy, with approximately 1.8 million cruise passengers arriving annually.

The island municipality's economy is primarily residential and tourism-based. Illa Daurada's luxury hotels, private beaches, and marina cater to high-income domestic and international visitors, while the island's permanent residents are largely professionals working in the mainland city who commute by ferry.

== Transport ==

{{see also|Metro de Sant Cristòfor|Transport in Balboa}}

[[File:Metro de Sant Cristofor train.png|thumb|right|300px|A train on the Metro de Sant Cristòfor elevated line.]]

[[Sant Cristòfor]] operates the [[Metro de Sant Cristòfor]], a rapid transit system consisting of nine lines opened between the 1980s and 2024. Two of the system's lines incorporate tunnels and infrastructure originally built during the Zong Balboa period. The metro serves the central city, the canal corridor, and the northern and southern suburbs, and carries approximately 1.2 million passengers daily. The system connects with the [[Areza Metro]] at the southern terminus of one of its lines.

The comarca is served by [[Intercon]], the regional rail operator, which provides commuter and intercity services from Sant Cristòfor. Intercon operates high-speed rail services between Sant Cristòfor and the International City of Areza and between Sant Cristòfor and Portnou.

The [[Ferries de la Badia de Sant Cristòfor]] connect the island municipality to the mainland waterfront.

Streetcar systems operate in the city centre, and the [[Port of Sant Cristòfor]], on the Shendan coast, is the country's principal port and one of the busiest in Lurandia.

== History ==

{{main|History of Balboa}}

[[File:Fort Sant Cristofor colonial era.png|thumb|right|300px|The original Volisanian fort at Sant Cristòfor, overlooking the Badia de Sant Cristòfor.]]

The Volisanian colonial administration established a fortified settlement on the Shendan coast in the sixteenth century, taking advantage of the sheltered anchorage of the Badia de Sant Cristòfor. The original fort, the '''Fort de Sant Cristòfor''', occupied the hill above the bay's southern shore and commanded the bay entrance. The settlement grew slowly around the fort, serving as a military outpost and naval supply station for the Volisanian colonial fleet.

Following the [[First War of the Isthmus]], Sant Cristòfor was designated a free port, and the city developed rapidly as a cosmopolitan entrepôt outside the plantation system. The free port status attracted merchants, sailors, and freedmen from across the Aethiopian and Shendan oceans, and the city became the colony's most economically significant settlement. The Districte Vell preserves the street pattern and many of the buildings from this period.

The [[Second War of the Isthmus]] (1893–1899) resulted in the loss of Sant Cristòfor and the canal zone to [[Zong]]. The Zong period (1899–1950) brought significant Fosian immigration and infrastructure investment, including the construction of the original two-line metro system, the redevelopment of the port district, and the establishment of the Districte Fosià as the administrative and commercial centre of Zong Balboa. The Zong administration also expanded the quarantine station on Illa del Portal and developed Illa Daurada as a residential enclave for Zongese officials and businessmen.

The [[Adonian Community]] assumed administration of the former Zong territory in 1950. The AC mandate period saw the reconstruction of the city's infrastructure, the expansion of the port, and the beginnings of the financial services sector. In 1964, the islands were separated from the mainland municipality to form the Municipalitat de les Illes.

The [[Treaty of Balboa Harbour]] (1960) provided for the return of the Sant Cristòfor mandate, subject to the [[Twelve Guarantees]]. The [[Treaty of Areza]] (1973) transferred the canal zone, completing the city's reunification with the rest of Balboa. The post-reunification period saw rapid urbanisation, the expansion of the metro system, the development of the financial district, and the transformation of the former docklands into mixed-use residential and commercial areas.

